WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:19.333
- So I wanna thank you again
for coming to this event.

00:00:20.214 --> 00:00:22.410
To us at MIT.nano, we are
looking to reach across

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:25.510
the boundaries of what you
might have seen before.

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:29.600
Indeed Matthew, when
you mentioned the order

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.870
and yet noise down the nanoscale,

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:35.760
that's indeed what the MIT.nano
logo was meant to symbolize.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.750
There is always periodicity that you find

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:40.010
when you look down at the atomic scale,

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:42.390
but you also find a great,
great deal of disorder

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.390
in a heights positions, orientations

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:47.770
like those nanowires John
Hart was showing before.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.980
There is that dichotomy
between perfect and imperfect,

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:52.630
and we always have to manage it.

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:54.840
And those scale understandings
will allow us indeed

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.610
to take the next steps towards discovery.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:00:58.840
So what I want to show you

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.090
was the things you can
discover inside MIT.nano.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:02.960
I'm just gonna spend a few minutes

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.750
showing you some images and
reasonings in the way we think

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:09.560
about designing the spaces
that ended up being MIT.nano.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:12.460
There was a fair bit
of thought and decision

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:16.450
that was purposeful as we
looked at how to make a space,

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:18.290
to make the next set of inventions

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:20.250
and hence take the mantle of what is real,

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:21.083
well it's given to us,

00:01:21.083 --> 00:01:23.980
which is redefine what next technology

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.040
and what next world will need.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.960
Well, that meant better
understand innovation

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.160
and innovation we realized,

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.560
comes from very simple, three things.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:34.510
People who have different perspectives,

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:36.630
hence are from different disciplines,

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.420
make sure those people
can meet each other,

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:41.890
and lastly, make sure those
people can engage each other

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:46.090
so that exchanges of ideas
can be fruitful to both.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:48.890
Ideas then was to generate a space

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:51.770
as central to the MIT campus
as we can get away with.

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:53.520
This is right next to the dome.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.520
It's connected to every other
building in the neighborhood

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.840
and extremely easy to reach.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.840
Oh, and make sure no one
feels like the sole owner

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:02.880
of this space.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:06.600
So make sure no professor
ever has an office

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:07.753
inside MIT.nano.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:11.670
Indeed, the only offices
you'll find inside

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:15.670
are those of staff members
who run the facility

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:18.290
for the benefit of the entire campus.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:20.200
Indeed, it was designed with this in mind,

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.110
in order to launch the
next set of innovations

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:23.790
we might be able to build.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:27.000
And so we took pen to
paper and started drawing,

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.480
picture emerged of what
piping at least should be.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.380
Well, this is CAD generated.

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:35.940
We did actually design the entire building

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:37.580
down to quarter of an inch

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.720
and it was assembled parts of it offsite

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.960
and then brought back
in, in order to be able

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:45.330
to be put into what ended up MIT.nano.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:48.110
The heart of it is clean
spaces down in the basement.

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:49.780
There is imaging spaces that give you

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.820
a lot of visualization
capability like never before

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:53.970
on this campus.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:56.000
And then up on the top
is the ways to launch

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.100
those discoveries into prototypes

00:02:58.100 --> 00:02:59.810
and hence companies, technologies

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:01.970
that could be given to the world.

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:04.250
We also looked at spaces
around the MIT.nano

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.740
realizing that we are
right next to the dome,

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:09.500
we have the responsibility
of telling a story

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:11.050
of what MIT is.

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:15.830
Not just used as facility to
discover the next set of ideas,

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:19.600
hence, we generated a
courtyard through it.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.540
So-called Improbability Walk runs.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:23.880
Why improbability Walk?

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.350
Well, it runs parallel
to the infinite corridor

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:28.430
and there is a staircase at the end

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:31.010
of the Improbability Walk
that connects the two.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.920
I think of it as the staircase

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:34.963
of infinite improbability, right?

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:37.540
But that being said,

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:39.360
there is a real reason why we called it

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:40.270
the Improbability Walk

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:42.220
and that is to commemorate
the improbable journeys

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:44.840
of all of us of actually having
a chance to meet each other

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:47.320
and discover the next set of discoveries

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:49.970
needed to launch forward the world.

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:52.362
That thought really was brought to us

00:03:52.362 --> 00:03:54.450
through inspirations like this.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:57.020
Our friends who unfortunately passed away,

00:03:57.020 --> 00:03:59.520
Millie Dresselhaus are renowned scientist,

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:02.430
incredible woman who has
redefined the boundaries

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:05.470
of what could be possible
in the nanoscience.

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:07.010
Has indeed found her journey

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:08.940
to be an incredibly impossibility.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.790
My background is so
improbable that I'd come here

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:14.340
to work from where I started
as a poor kid in the Bronx.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:17.600
And she particularly
appreciated walking along

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.810
with her students who are
younger peers of faculty,

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:25.050
post-docs because she learned
her own way of discovery

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.890
by being a young graduate
student University of Chicago

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:29.970
and having happened to have lived

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:32.530
in the same neighborhood as Enrico Fermi.

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:34.710
Hence, taking walks in the morning

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.880
with a very famous scientist in the Decaux

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:39.210
all the way to the university,
and in those walks,

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:41.500
she said she learned
how to be a scientist.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:43.770
She discovered through the interactions,

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:45.820
through the cross-pollination of ideas.

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:48.030
And what does it mean
to take the next step

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:50.050
in the discovery of the world?

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.947
Now, as a result, we looked at MIT.nano

00:04:52.947 --> 00:04:55.040
and the spaces around it as spaces

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:56.720
that should enable gatherings.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.490
There is the staircase
of infinite improbability

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:02.370
with a singing group on top of it,

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:03.940
because we wanna make sure we use it

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:06.600
as a performing space not just as a space

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:08.680
that is designated for
science and technology.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:10.850
And the courtyard looked the other way,

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:12.120
on the left is MIT.nano,

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:13.850
on the right are the password buildings

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:16.090
of the original MIT campus.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:18.740
On the left where MIT.nano
is that whole length,

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:22.450
50 linear feet, pardon
me, a hundred linear feet

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:25.390
on the ground floor and the
floor above are galleries.

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:28.180
Galleries that can
actually provide you art

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:30.800
and provide you ways of
telling stories of connection

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:34.100
of what we do on campus
to the society at large.

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.349
The building itself,

00:05:35.349 --> 00:05:38.560
you might notice has elements
of design in it as well.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:42.400
The frit on the windows, I
have been trying to figure out

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:45.373
Kim use it as a binary
code to spell messages.

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:47.650
I'll leave it to you to decipher

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:49.940
if there is a message or not.

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:52.480
I'm sure some of the undergrads
who already gone through

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:54.360
to figure this one out,

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:57.180
but there are views throughout the space

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:00.330
that allow you to see the
space in very different ways.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:02.200
We have purposely left the windows open

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.570
so that those inside can see
the birches on the right,

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:08.020
because seeing the natural
world is an inspiration

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:10.520
for what is happening inside the MIT.nano.

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:14.280
Perfectly clean spaces is
a very comforting thing.

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:16.150
You realize what you're working towards

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:19.230
of you have the world to reflect upon.

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:21.300
Indeed, art spaces like this,

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:25.910
we see as expressions
of artists, builders,

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:29.180
who have indeed thought of
how to reduce the complexity

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:31.330
of what needs to be done inside MIT.nano

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:33.130
into an inspirational moment.

00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:35.927
Hence this, these are views
from the windows of MIT

00:06:35.927 --> 00:06:37.590
and in the looking into the machinery

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:39.400
that operates the building.

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:41.180
But also throughout the space,

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:45.830
you will find canvases
like the big orange wall

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:47.100
that goes vertically.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:48.980
That's a canvas five stories tall

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:51.520
that could be painted with years to come

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:53.130
with discovery after discovery

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:55.700
or the next set of ideas
that we might wanna go ahead

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:58.490
and explore or project onto the people.

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:02.200
Indeed, I like to say
that limestone on the left

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:03.470
that is dressing the walls

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:06.470
that (indistinct) the MIT.nano clean room,

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:08.830
that limestone, well, the order for it,

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:12.400
we placed 400 million years ago

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:14.570
because that's when it
was started to be made.

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:17.960
It's a limestone made
of a Louisville deposits

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:19.470
over 400 million years.

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:22.280
I'm sure there are a little
micro and nano organisms

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:24.333
staring back at us from those walls.

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:27.300
We are impressed by the nature itself

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:30.310
and seek inspiration and
hence have dressed MIT.nano

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:31.990
with a lot of natural elements

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:35.260
to allow us to take the next
steps of how to discover.

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:38.990
Now, also we have provided
plentiful gathering spaces

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:41.060
throughout the building
in order to make sure

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:43.900
that you can go ahead and
indeed have that moment

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:46.460
of discovery with someone you encountered.

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:48.060
But also throughout the building,

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:49.600
you're gonna start finding art.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:52.698
So if you're down on the
first floor on the left,

00:07:52.698 --> 00:07:55.130
we show you a piece, we call One.MIT,

00:07:55.130 --> 00:07:57.870
I'll tell you about it in a moment,

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:00.510
and then a continuation of that right now

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:04.480
is dressed with a digital
display of 16 TV screens,

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.180
running 16 simultaneous videos

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:09.083
from the studio Olafur Eliasson.

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:13.600
This tells stories in a very vivid way

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:15.640
of the way he makes his art

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:18.120
so that when you go and
see the Northwest passage,

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:21.190
the piece of art dressing
in the MIT.nano breezeway,

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:23.020
you can even understand the thinkings

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:25.370
of the artist as he goes forward.

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:27.880
This digital world gallery
allows us to take also

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:30.120
the next steps in energizing the campus

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:33.090
in providing us the next
exhibit and the next exhibit.

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:36.560
And by the way, what
is glowing back at you

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:38.290
are organic molecules

00:08:38.290 --> 00:08:41.230
because these are so
called organic LED displays

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:44.770
that operate by using
hundred molecular films.

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:46.140
10 molecules in the middle glow

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:47.690
to give you the colors that you see,

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:49.560
the reds, the greens and the blues,

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:51.430
and the blacks are perfectly deep.

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:53.630
Consequently giving the
largest contrast ratio

00:08:53.630 --> 00:08:54.840
between on and off.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:57.550
Very good looking TVs, just saying.

00:08:57.550 --> 00:08:59.640
All right, that being said,

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:04.640
One.MIT as an art piece is
a symbol of the MIT campus.

00:09:05.912 --> 00:09:09.350
The quintessential view,
the iconic view of the MIT

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:11.740
is the view of the MIT great dome,

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:14.010
but the MIT great dome is only great

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:18.190
because of who people are
that indeed have walked

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:21.120
through corridors of MIT
to make it as important

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:23.140
as it ended up being today.

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:26.660
So we might as well represented
that way by going ahead

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:29.210
and taking a closer look into the dome

00:09:29.210 --> 00:09:33.380
only to realize that the
dome actually consists

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:36.760
of names either bolded or non-bolded

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:39.100
to generate the image you observe.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:42.960
Now, you might notice that
there is website listed down

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:44.800
on the bottom lower left.

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:49.057
As of today, you can go to
that website, onemit.mit.edu.

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:54.900
And if you wanna find out
where among the 270,000 names

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:58.780
that happen to be the number
of names that compose the image

00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:00.150
where your name might be,

00:10:00.150 --> 00:10:02.650
'cause you might have been a student,

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:06.380
a professor, a staff,
a visiting scientist,

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:11.140
a Lincoln lab employee,
anyone between 1861 and 2018,

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:15.420
who was in some way associated with MIT

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:18.070
and then found its way
in the MIT directory,

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:21.920
that intellect has been
forever more remembered

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:23.800
in One.MIT art piece.

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:26.440
And indeed what we've
done is taken at five foot

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:29.600
by five foot image and
shrunk it down to a six inch

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:32.600
by six inch silicon wafer etched that way

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:34.380
with all those names.

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:36.770
And they've mounted it
onto the walls of MIT.nano

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:40.260
so that the 270,000 names of all those

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:42.960
who provide intellectual history of MIT

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:46.410
have forevermore been
indeed etched in stone

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:49.530
and memorialized on the walls of MIT.nano.

00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:53.160
The smallest line needed
to write the narrow parts

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:56.900
of the letters is four microns.

00:10:56.900 --> 00:10:58.860
That's 4,000 nanometers.

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:02.090
From our perspective,
huge, huge dimensions

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:06.350
and the wafer itself has I made at MIT

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:10.590
over in the building 39,
the present Micro-Nano fab.

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:14.340
And it could be in the years
to come remade in MIT.nano

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:19.030
and we will do that in order
to aggregate additional names.

00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:21.440
This is another art piece
to see inside the building.

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:23.540
It's has been a gift of the class of 2018,

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:25.197
who worked with us as we developed it.

00:11:25.197 --> 00:11:29.280
And professor Craig Carter
led the way in laying out

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:31.160
the wafer and then organizing a team

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:34.180
with a set of people like any wing

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:36.000
from the special projects of MIT.nano,

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:39.520
who indeed organized us
into making this happen.

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:42.070
On the second floor of MIT.nano galleries,

00:11:42.070 --> 00:11:43.380
you can see micro mosaics,

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:47.880
an art form that was
perfected about 250 years ago.

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:50.730
What human hand could do
with the art of the time

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:55.210
was essentially take
pieces of glass or sand

00:11:55.210 --> 00:11:57.720
that were double the size of a human hair,

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:01.210
human hair width and make an image.

00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:03.110
We indeed have a privilege of having some

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:05.350
of those micro mosaic pieces
and we have gone ahead

00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:08.880
and zoomed in to take
details of the artistry

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:09.910
and expand them.

00:12:09.910 --> 00:12:11.670
So if you walk on the
second floor gallery,

00:12:11.670 --> 00:12:14.980
you'll see an expanded views
of little pieces this big

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:16.610
blown to six foot sizes.

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:19.250
Again, in order to be able
to show you the miracle

00:12:19.250 --> 00:12:23.300
of what human hand can do with
the tools of 250 years ago.

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:25.120
So just imagine what we'll be able to do

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:27.680
with the tools of MIT.nano.

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:29.630
Now, if you keep on going
on the second floor,

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:32.040
you will see the work of Berenice Abbott.

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:34.240
This is an exhibit of art pieces.

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:36.200
She was a photographer
who happened to have spent

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:40.920
a year with MIT back in 1957-58.

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:43.550
And she would take photographs of cities.

00:12:43.550 --> 00:12:45.150
But when she came to MIT,

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:48.530
she realized that opportunity
of capturing science

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:50.620
in ways that have never been done before.

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:53.770
The exhibit was provided
to us by the List Gallery

00:12:53.770 --> 00:12:57.260
who made us recognize the
opportunity of juxtaposing

00:12:57.260 --> 00:12:59.600
her city work with her science work

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:02.150
and makes I think a very stunning exhibit.

00:13:02.150 --> 00:13:04.500
Please check it out.

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:06.510
You can then go to the
third floor of MIT.nano

00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:11.310
and actually immerse yourself
in the Spider Canvas exhibit.

00:13:11.310 --> 00:13:15.623
It is in the immersion lab
and it's active right now.

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:18.990
You can think of it as
if the described to me

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:23.990
as having a harp with 2,700 strings,

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:28.290
or is it more than that? 270,000 strings?

00:13:28.290 --> 00:13:30.470
Every piece of the spiderweb indeed

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:32.050
can generate a different sound,

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:34.100
just like Marcus was telling us.

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:36.500
And as a result, every one of them

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:39.143
can be immersive experience
for you to fly through.

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:44.530
It's then projected on a
beautiful streamed cube,

00:13:44.530 --> 00:13:47.623
hence giving a both the visual
and then auditory stimulus.

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:51.560
So MIT.nano is further than dressed

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:53.120
with this final image I'm gonna show you,

